    <body>
      <sec id="article-27468.s1" sec-type="Continuing Education Activity">
        <title>Continuing Education Activity</title>
        <p>Postinfarction ventricular septal rupture (VSR) is a rare but catastrophic mechanical complication of acute myocardial infarction characterized by rupture of the interventricular septum, resulting in an abnormal left-to-right shunt between the ventricles.&#x000a0;This condition typically occurs within the first several days following a transmural infarction and is most often associated with delayed or absent reperfusion therapy. The rupture results from ischemic myocardial necrosis, inflammatory degradation of septal tissue, and hemodynamic stress on the weakened myocardium. Clinically, patients may initially appear stable but can rapidly deteriorate with acute heart failure, pulmonary edema, hypotension, and cardiogenic shock. A new harsh holosystolic murmur along the lower left sternal border is a classic finding. Prompt diagnosis, most commonly with transthoracic echocardiography, and urgent multidisciplinary management, often requiring surgical repair or percutaneous intervention, are critical to improving survival.</p>
        <p>Clinicians participating in this course gain a comprehensive understanding of the epidemiology, pathophysiology, clinical presentation, diagnostic evaluation, and management of postinfarction VSR. The activity emphasizes early recognition of mechanical complications following myocardial infarction, appropriate use of imaging and hemodynamic monitoring, and differentiation of VSR from other postmyocardial infarction mechanical complications such as papillary muscle rupture or free wall rupture. Participants will also review current surgical and percutaneous repair strategies and learn the importance of coordinated care among cardiology, cardiothoracic surgery, and critical care teams to optimize patient outcomes in this life-threatening condition.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Objectives:</bold>
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Identify the risk factors, timing, and clinical features associated with postinfarction ventricular septal rupture following acute myocardial infarction.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Differentiate postinfarction ventricular septal rupture from other mechanical complications of myocardial infarction, including papillary muscle rupture with acute mitral regurgitation and left ventricular free wall rupture with cardiac tamponade.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Select appropriate treatment strategies, including surgical repair or percutaneous closure, based on patient stability, defect characteristics, and institutional expertise.</p></list-item><list-item><p>Collaborate with cardiology, cardiothoracic surgery, and critical care specialists to develop an individualized management plan.</p></list-item></list>
<ext-link xmlns:xlink="http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink" ext-link-type="uri" xlink:href="https://www.statpearls.com/account/trialuserreg/?articleid=27468&#x00026;utm_source=pubmed&#x00026;utm_campaign=reviews&#x00026;utm_content=27468">Access free multiple choice questions on this topic.</ext-link>
</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s2" sec-type="pubmed-excerpt">
        <title>Introduction</title>
        <p>Ventricular septal rupture (VSR) is one of the most dramatic and life-threatening complications of acute myocardial infarction. This rare complication can occur in both ST-elevation myocardial infarction (STEMI) and non&#x02013;ST-elevation myocardial infarction (NSTEMI), although it's less common in NSTEMI. VSR is characterized by tearing or rupture of acutely infarcted myocardial tissue, forming a defect in the interventricular septum.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r1">[1]</xref> The clinical characteristics of this complication vary with the infarct location and the site of rupture, which may be anterior or posterior VSR. Post-myocardial infarction VSR is a dreaded complication and confers high morbidity and mortality in all patients.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s3" sec-type="Etiology">
        <title>Etiology</title>
        <p>Postinfarct VSR is a catastrophic mechanical complication of acute myocardial infarction caused by prolonged, unrelieved ischemia leading to transmural myocardial necrosis and structural failure of the interventricular septum. The rupture results from enzymatic degradation and macrophage-mediated resorption of necrotic myocardium, creating a friable septal substrate that ultimately tears under intraventricular pressure. VSR most commonly develops 2 to 8 days after an acute or first&#x000a0;myocardial infarction in the setting of delayed or absent reperfusion therapy, though it may occur within 1 to 14 days post-STEMI.</p>
        <p>The incidence of this condition demonstrates a bimodal distribution, with peaks within the first 24 hours and again at 3 to 5 days postinfarction. With advances in percutaneous coronary intervention, the overall incidence has declined substantially. Angiography typically demonstrates acute occlusion of a single coronary vessel supplying the infarct territory, often without collateral circulation, which predisposes to full-thickness necrosis and rupture.</p>
        <p>Anterior septal rupture accounts for approximately 60% of cases and is most commonly associated with occlusion of the left anterior descending artery. In contrast, posterior septal rupture (approximately 40%) is typically linked to right coronary artery occlusion, particularly when it supplies the posterior descending artery. The resulting septal defect permits acute left-to-right shunting, producing a new harsh pansystolic murmur, marked increase in pulmonary blood flow, diffuse pulmonary edema, reduced systemic perfusion, and rapid hemodynamic deterioration.</p>
        <p>Right ventricular volume overload and failure frequently ensue, often progressing to cardiogenic shock. VSR is more likely in older patients, women, those with hypertension, chronic kidney disease, and individuals experiencing a first&#x000a0;myocardial infarction without prior collateral development. Conversely, patients with diabetes mellitus, chronic angina, prior myocardial infarction, or a history of smoking, conditions often associated with collateral formation, appear less likely to develop rupture. Despite its decreased frequency in the reperfusion era, postinfarct VSR remains a life-threatening event requiring urgent recognition and intervention.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s4" sec-type="Epidemiology">
        <title>Epidemiology</title>
        <p>Postmyocardial infarction VSR is an uncommon but devastating mechanical complication of acute myocardial infarction, now occurring in approximately 0.2% to 0.3% of all acute myocardial infarctions in the contemporary reperfusion era.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r2">[2]</xref> In patients with STEMI who do not receive reperfusion therapy, the incidence ranges from 1% to 3%; this decreases to 0.2% to 0.34% in those treated with fibrinolytic therapy.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r4">[4]</xref> Among patients who undergo reperfusion, VSR occurs more frequently after fibrinolytic therapy than after primary percutaneous coronary intervention (PCI), reflecting more effective infarct size limitation with timely PCI.</p>
        <p>The incidence rises markedly, to 3.9%, in patients who develop cardiogenic shock as a complication of STEMI.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r3">[3]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r4">[4]</xref> Historically reported at 1% to 2% prior to routine reperfusion, the overall decline in VSR incidence is attributable to advances in early revascularization strategies. Despite this reduction, VSR remains associated with high mortality, particularly in patients with delayed presentation, extensive transmural infarction, advanced age, female sex, anterior myocardial infarction, or single-vessel coronary disease without collateral circulation.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s5" sec-type="Pathophysiology">
        <title>Pathophysiology</title>
        <p>The pathophysiology of postinfarction VSR is driven by a sequence of structural and hemodynamic changes following transmural myocardial infarction. The initiating event is prolonged coronary occlusion leading to myocardial necrosis and tissue weakening within the interventricular septum. Following infarction, coagulative necrosis develops, followed by enzymatic degradation of structural proteins and macrophage-mediated resorption of necrotic tissue. During this period, the infarcted myocardium becomes progressively thin, friable, and mechanically unstable. This process is further exacerbated by infarct expansion, in which thinning and dilation of the infarct zone increase wall stress and distort the septal architecture, predisposing the necrotic tissue to mechanical failure.</p>
        <p>The inflammatory response that accompanies infarct healing also contributes to structural vulnerability. Neutrophil infiltration and proteolytic enzyme activity degrade extracellular matrix components before adequate collagen deposition and scar formation can occur. As a result, the septum temporarily lacks structural integrity during the early postinfarction period. Superimposed hemodynamic stress,&#x000a0;particularly a high pressure gradient between the left and right ventricles, places additional mechanical strain on the weakened septum, ultimately leading to rupture. Once the septum fails, a large left-to-right shunt forms, causing acute increases in pulmonary blood flow, right ventricular volume overload, reduced systemic cardiac output, and rapid progression to pulmonary edema and cardiogenic shock.</p>
        <p>The presence or absence of collateral circulation and the resulting infarct size also play a critical role in the development of VSR. Patients with well-developed collateral vessels often experience smaller infarcts and partial preservation of septal perfusion, which reduces the likelihood of full-thickness necrosis and rupture. Conversely, patients with a first&#x000a0;myocardial infarction and limited collateral circulation are more prone to extensive transmural infarction and septal destruction. Larger infarcts therefore produce greater structural weakening and higher intraventricular stress, substantially increasing the risk of VSR.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s6" sec-type="Histopathology">
        <title>Histopathology</title>
        <p>The histopathology of postinfarction VSR&#x000a0;reflects the structural breakdown of infarcted myocardial tissue during the early phases of infarct healing. VSR almost always occurs in the setting of a transmural myocardial infarction, where full-thickness necrosis involves the interventricular septum. The rupture typically develops at the border zone between infarcted and viable myocardium, an area particularly susceptible to mechanical failure due to the juxtaposition of weakened necrotic tissue and contracting viable myocardium. During the first several days after infarction, neutrophil infiltration and macrophage-mediated phagocytosis remove necrotic myocytes and degrade the extracellular matrix, resulting in a soft, friable myocardium that lacks tensile strength. Before significant collagen deposition and scar formation occur, the infarcted septum becomes vulnerable to tearing under normal intracardiac pressure gradients.</p>
        <p>The location and morphology of the septal defect correlate with the infarct territory. In anterior wall myocardial infarction, rupture most commonly occurs in the apical portion of the interventricular septum, whereas inferior wall infarctions more frequently produce defects in the basal posterior septum. The size of the rupture can vary considerably, ranging from small defects to openings several centimeters long. Morphologically, the defect may be direct and discrete, forming a relatively straight communication between the ventricles, or irregular and serpiginous, particularly when the rupture tracks through areas of heterogeneous necrosis. Multiple fenestrations are more commonly observed with inferior infarctions, creating complex channels through the septal tissue.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r5">[5]</xref>&#x000a0;These structural patterns influence both hemodynamic severity and the technical complexity of surgical or percutaneous repair.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s7" sec-type="History and Physical">
        <title>History and Physical</title>
        <p>Patients with postinfarction VSR often have a recent history of acute&#x000a0;myocardial infarction and may initially appear clinically stable before experiencing sudden deterioration. Early in the disease course, some patients may seem relatively comfortable; however, recurrence of angina, progressive dyspnea, pulmonary edema, hypotension, and cardiogenic shock may subsequently develop abruptly. In other cases, the first manifestation may be sudden hemodynamic compromise characterized by hypotension, biventricular failure, and a new loud systolic murmur.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r4">[4]</xref> The abrupt development of a left-to-right intracardiac shunt results in increased pulmonary blood flow, reduced systemic cardiac output, and rapid progression to respiratory distress and circulatory collapse. Symptoms frequently include worsening shortness of breath, fatigue, diaphoresis, orthopnea, and signs of low cardiac output, such as pulmonary congestion and right ventricular volume overload.</p>
        <p>On physical examination,&#x000a0;a&#x000a0;new pansystolic (holosystolic) murmur&#x000a0;should strongly suggest the diagnosis&#x000a0;in a patient with recent myocardial infarction, particularly in the setting of worsening hemodynamics or biventricular failure. Careful and repeated cardiac examinations are essential in all patients with acute&#x000a0;myocardial infarction to identify this complication early. The murmur of acute postmyocardial infarction VSR is typically best heard at the lower left sternal border and is accompanied by a palpable systolic thrill in approximately 50% of cases.</p>
        <p>The murmur often radiates across the precordium and may be associated with tachycardia, hypotension, elevated jugular venous pressure, pulmonary crackles, and signs of systemic hypoperfusion. Importantly, in patients with large septal defects and severe heart failure or cardiogenic shock, the murmur may be faint or even inaudible due to reduced pressure gradients across the ventricles; therefore, the absence of a murmur does not exclude VSR. Continuous hemodynamic monitoring and a high index of suspicion are therefore essential for timely diagnosis and intervention.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s8" sec-type="Evaluation">
        <title>Evaluation</title>
        <p>Evaluation of suspected postmyocardial infarction VSR requires rapid clinical assessment, targeted laboratory testing, hemodynamic monitoring, and imaging studies to confirm the diagnosis and differentiate it from other mechanical complications of myocardial infarction. On physical examination, clinicians should look for signs of sudden clinical deterioration and cardiogenic shock, including hypotension, tachycardia, cool extremities, altered mentation, and evidence of poor systemic perfusion. Patients may demonstrate worsening pulmonary status with rales or respiratory distress, reduced urine output, and rising serum lactate reflecting tissue hypoperfusion. A new harsh pansystolic murmur, typically best heard along the lower left sternal border, is a key diagnostic clue. When evaluating these findings, the differential diagnosis should include VSR, acute papillary muscle rupture causing severe mitral regurgitation, and left ventricular free wall rupture with cardiac tamponade, all of which can present with rapid hemodynamic compromise.</p>
        <p>Initial diagnostic evaluation includes electrocardiography, which confirms the presence of an acute or recent&#x000a0;STEMI and may reveal atrioventricular or infra-nodal conduction abnormalities in approximately 40% of patients with VSR. Transthoracic echocardiography is the diagnostic test of choice, allowing direct visualization of the septal defect, identification of the rupture's location and size, and estimation of the magnitude of the left-to-right shunt using Doppler imaging.</p>
        <p>Basal septal defects are best visualized in the parasternal long-axis view with medial angulation, the apical long-axis view, and the subcostal long-axis view. In contrast, apical septal ruptures are best seen in the apical 4-chamber view. Echocardiography also provides essential information on ventricular function, a major determinant of prognosis, and guides therapeutic decision-making.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r4">[4]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r6">[6]</xref> If transthoracic imaging is nondiagnostic, transesophageal echocardiography may be required for improved visualization.</p>
        <p>Hemodynamic evaluation with right heart catheterization can further support the diagnosis by demonstrating a characteristic oxygen saturation step-up between the right atrium and right ventricle, or between the right ventricle and pulmonary artery, indicating a left-to-right intracardiac shunt. This finding is particularly helpful in differentiating VSR from acute postinfarction mitral regurgitation, in which such an oxygen step-up is absent. Left heart catheterization with ventriculography may also identify the septal defect, particularly when performed in the left anterior oblique projection with cranial angulation. Advanced imaging modalities such as cardiac magnetic resonance imaging or cardiac computed tomography can provide a detailed anatomic assessment; however, their use is limited in the acute setting because many patients are hemodynamically unstable. Together, these diagnostic modalities enable rapid confirmation of VSR, assessment of defect anatomy, and planning for urgent surgical or percutaneous intervention.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s9" sec-type="Treatment / Management">
        <title>Treatment / Management</title>
        <p>Patients with suspected postmyocardial infarction VSR should be recognized early and be treated in an intensive care unit due to the high risk of rapid hemodynamic deterioration. Invasive hemodynamic monitoring is generally recommended to guide management. Placement of an arterial line and a pulmonary artery (Swan&#x02013;Ganz) catheter allows continuous monitoring of systemic pressures, right- and left-sided filling pressures, and cardiac output. These measurements are essential for guiding fluid management, diuretic therapy, and vasoactive support. Calculation of systemic vascular resistance&#x000a0;from cardiac output and mean arterial pressure can help guide vasodilator therapy.</p>
        <p>In patients with a systolic blood pressure greater than 90 mm Hg, vasodilators such as nitroglycerin or sodium nitroprusside should be initiated as soon as hemodynamic monitoring is established to reduce afterload and decrease the magnitude of the left-to-right shunt. Inotropic agents may also be required to maintain adequate cardiac output. These measures are often necessary to stabilize the patient before definitive diagnostic evaluation and repair. If pharmacologic therapy is insufficient or poorly tolerated, intra-aortic balloon pump counterpulsation should be considered to improve coronary perfusion and reduce afterload.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r7">[7]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r8">[8]</xref></p>
        <p>When hemodynamic instability persists, mechanical circulatory support may be required. Extracorporeal membrane oxygenation (ECMO) can provide temporary circulatory support, reduce myocardial workload, and stabilize end-organ perfusion prior to definitive repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r9">[9]</xref> Mechanical ventilation may also be necessary in patients with worsening pulmonary edema or respiratory distress. Patients presenting with cardiogenic shock should be medically stabilized before escalation to advanced mechanical circulatory support whenever possible. Although no universal management algorithm exists for postinfarction VSR, 2 key principles include reducing the magnitude of the left-to-right shunt and preventing ongoing cardiogenic shock.</p>
        <p>Emergency surgical repair in the setting of profound shock carries an extremely high operative mortality. Increasing clinical experience suggests that outcomes may improve when temporary mechanical circulatory support allows time for partial infarct healing and fibrosis, providing more durable tissue for surgical repair. Veno-arterial ECMO is often used for this purpose and can be initiated at the bedside or in the operating room to restore systemic perfusion and correct end-organ malperfusion. In selected cases, the addition of a temporary left ventricular assist device may further unload the left ventricle, decrease the magnitude of the shunt, and reduce pulmonary congestion.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r10">[10]</xref></p>
        <p>Despite advances in supportive therapy, urgent surgical closure remains the definitive treatment for postinfarction VSR. Some clinicians advocate delaying surgery to allow friable infarcted tissue to mature and potentially reduce operative mortality; however, the reported survival benefit may largely reflect selection bias, as patients stable enough to tolerate delay often represent a less critically ill population. Among patients treated medically without surgical repair, mortality is extremely high, approximately 24% within 72 hours and up to 75% by 3 weeks.</p>
        <p>Operative mortality also varies by infarct location, reaching approximately 70% in inferior myocardial infarction compared with 30% in anterior infarctions. Inferior defects are often more technically challenging and are frequently associated with concomitant mitral regurgitation, sometimes requiring simultaneous mitral valve repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r3">[3]</xref> Although surgery remains the gold-standard treatment, percutaneous closure techniques are increasingly used in carefully selected patients, particularly those with prohibitive surgical risk or who are awaiting definitive repair.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r3">[3]</xref></p>
        <p>
<bold>Surgical Technique</bold>
</p>
        <p>Induction of general anesthesia must be performed cautiously in patients with postmyocardial infarction VSR, as aggressive induction can precipitate acute hemodynamic collapse. The patient is positioned supine on the operating table with both arms tucked, prepped, and draped in the standard sterile fashion. A median sternotomy is performed to access the mediastinum.</p>
        <p>The pericardium is opened, and systemic heparinization is administered. The ascending aorta and right atrium are cannulated, and after an adequate activated clotting time is achieved, cardiopulmonary bypass&#x000a0;is initiated. The left ventricle is vented through the right superior pulmonary vein. An aortic cross-clamp is then applied, and myocardial protection is achieved with antegrade and retrograde cardioplegia.</p>
        <p>Successful surgical repair depends primarily on complete closure and exclusion of the septal defect. Several repair techniques have been described; however, the infarct exclusion technique described by Dr Tirone David is widely favored and can be applied to both anterior and posterior defects. If coronary artery bypass grafting is planned, distal graft anastomoses should be performed before ventriculotomy to minimize ischemic time and facilitate exposure.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Repair of an Anterior Defect</bold>
</p>
        <p>For anterior VSR, the left ventricle is opened near the apex, approximately 2 cm lateral to the left anterior descending (LAD) coronary artery, ensuring careful avoidance of the LAD. The incision is made through the infarcted region of the anterior ventricular wall. Once the ventricle is opened, traction sutures are placed along the ventricular myocardium to improve exposure of the septum. The infarcted septal tissue is typically friable and necrotic and should not be aggressively debrided, as the defect may consist of multiple irregular or serpiginous channels within the septum.</p>
        <p>The infarct exclusion technique is then used to isolate the septal defect from the left ventricular cavity. A large bovine pericardial patch is tailored and secured using a running 3-0 or 4-0 polypropylene (Prolene) suture. The patch is first anchored to viable myocardium in the basal region of the left ventricle beneath the mitral valve annulus.</p>
        <p>The suture line is then advanced toward the ventricular apex, ensuring that all sutures pass through healthy, noninfarcted ventricular muscle. Once the suture reaches the apex, it is brought full-thickness through the ventricular wall to the epicardial surface. A second suture line is then started again at the basal region near the mitral valve annulus, running along the anterior ventricular wall until it reaches the junction between the septum and the ventricular free wall. The suture is then brought through the ventricular wall.</p>
        <p>The patch must be sufficiently large to avoid excessive tension, which could lead to repair failure. The anterior ventricular wall portion of the repair is completed using full-thickness sutures reinforced with a felt strip along the leftward side of the ventriculotomy. This effectively excludes the septal rupture from the left ventricular cavity. The left ventriculotomy is then closed with a running suture reinforced with felt strips to provide additional structural support.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Repair of a Posterior Defect</bold>
</p>
        <p>Posterior VSR repair is technically more challenging&#x000a0;due to&#x000a0;limited exposure and&#x000a0;extensive inferior wall infarction. Care must be taken when manipulating the heart, as excessive traction can tear the infarcted ventricular wall. The apex of the heart is gently elevated superiorly and toward the patient&#x02019;s right side to expose the posterior left ventricular surface.</p>
        <p>The infarcted area is identified, and a ventriculotomy is created approximately 2 cm to the left of the posterior descending artery to avoid injury to the vessel. The incision is extended from the infarcted region toward the left ventricular apex. Traction sutures are again placed along the ventricular muscle to enhance exposure. At this stage, the septum lies to the patient&#x02019;s right, while the mitral valve chordae are visible to the patient&#x02019;s left.</p>
        <p>A pericardial patch is then secured using the infarct exclusion technique described above. The repair begins at the midpoint of the mitral valve annulus on the patient&#x02019;s left side, and the suture is run along the annulus toward the ventricular apex. Once the apex is reached, the sutures are brought through the epicardial surface of the heart and reinforced with a felt strip. This patch effectively excludes the septal rupture from the left ventricular cavity. The posterior ventriculotomy is then closed with a running suture reinforced with&#x000a0;2 felt strips to strengthen the repair.</p>
        <p>
<bold>Alternative Repair Techniques</bold>
</p>
        <p>An alternative approach uses a nitinol mesh septal occluder device. In this technique, the occluder device is positioned within the septal defect and secured. A pericardial patch is then placed over the occluder device, creating a reinforced scaffold that provides additional structural support to the repair and reduces the risk of residual shunting or patch failure.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r11">[11]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s10" sec-type="Differential Diagnosis">
        <title>Differential Diagnosis</title>
        <p>Distinguishing between acute mitral regurgitation and rupture of the ventricular septum in patients with STEMI who suddenly develop a loud systolic murmur can be challenging on clinical grounds. The most straightforward method to differentiate these conditions is color flow Doppler echocardiography. Additionally, right heart catheterization with a balloon-tipped catheter can easily distinguish between these two complications. Patients with VSR show an increase in oxygen saturation in blood samples from the right ventricle and pulmonary artery compared to those from the right atrium. In contrast, patients with acute mitral regurgitation do not exhibit this step-up and may show tall C-V waves in both the pulmonary capillary and pulmonary arterial pressure tracings.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r12">[12]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r13">[13]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s11" sec-type="Prognosis">
        <title>Prognosis</title>
        <p>The prognosis of postinfarction VSR is poor without timely intervention, with mortality rates exceeding 60% in untreated cases. Factors such as rupture location, cardiogenic shock, and timing of intervention strongly influence long-term survival.&#x000a0;Continued research and innovation in surgical techniques, mechanical circulatory support, and transcatheter therapies are essential to improve outcomes for this life-threatening condition.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r12">[12]</xref></p>
        <p>Factors influencing prognosis include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Hemodynamic status before the operation
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Cardiogenic shock and multiorgan failure are negative predictors.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Timing of surgical intervention
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>A delayed surgical intervention to resuscitate the patient and allow infarcted tissue to mature has a better outcome.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Complexity of defect
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Highly complex and large defects pose significant technical challenges for closure and carry a high risk of residual shunting or re-rupture.</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Concomitant diseases
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Concomitant diseases, including chronic kidney disease and pulmonary hypertension, worsen the surgical outcome.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r13">[13]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s12" sec-type="Complications">
        <title>Complications</title>
        <p>Complications of postinfarction VSR include:</p>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
            <p>Cardiogenic shock
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>This is the most frequent and serious VSR complication.&#x000a0;This complication is secondary to the heart&#x02019;s failure to generate the cardiac output required to maintain systemic perfusion, leading to end-organ damage. Cardiogenic shock is associated with almost uniformly high mortality, up to 90% to 95% in medically treated individuals and 19% to 60% in those who are surgically treated.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r14">[14]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Pulmonary hypertension and edema
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The left-to-right shunting of blood caused by VSR increases pulmonary blood flow and pressure, leading to pulmonary hypertension. This condition can progress to pulmonary edema, characterized by fluid accumulation in the lungs, causing severe respiratory distress. Pulmonary complications are particularly pronounced in patients with large septal defects or delayed diagnosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r15">[15]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Right ventricular failure
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>The increased workload on the right ventricle from a left-to-right shunt can lead to right ventricular failure. This condition further exacerbates systemic hypoperfusion and contributes to multi-organ dysfunction. Right ventricular failure is a critical determinant of poor outcomes in patients with VSR.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r16">[16]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Recurrent septal rupture
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Occasionally, the repaired fistula re-ruptures, leading to recurrence of VSR. This is more common in those with large myocardial necrosis or an incomplete surgical correction. Rupture after repeat imposes considerable therapeutic challenges and usually mandates reoperations.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r17">[17]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Arrhythmias
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Patients undergoing VSR carry a high risk of arrhythmogenesis (atrial fibrillation and ventricular tachycardia). These arrhythmias can also interfere with cardiac output and lead to sudden death. Patients with significant myocardial damage or pre-existing conduction system disorders have a particularly high risk of developing arrhythmias.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r18">[18]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Neurological issues
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Systemic embolization of thrombi formed in the left ventricle can lead to neurological complications such as stroke or transient ischemic attacks. These events are more likely in patients with large septal defects or prolonged periods of untreated VSR.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r14">[14]</xref>&#x000a0;</p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
          <list-item>
            <p>Infective endocarditis
<list list-type="bullet"><list-item><p>Infective endocarditis is a rare but serious complication of VSR. The turbulent blood flow across the septal defect creates a favorable environment for bacterial colonization, particularly in patients with prosthetic patches or devices used for repair. Prophylactic antibiotics are often recommended to reduce the risk of endocarditis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r19">[19]</xref></p></list-item></list>
</p>
          </list-item>
        </list>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s13" sec-type="Deterrence and Patient Education">
        <title>Deterrence and Patient Education</title>
        <p>Prevention of postmyocardial infarction&#x000a0;VSR relies primarily on early recognition and optimal management of acute myocardial infarction. Prompt diagnosis and risk stratification are essential to reduce the likelihood of mechanical complications. Patients presenting with suspected myocardial infarction should undergo an electrocardiogram to identify ST-elevation or non&#x02013;STEMI, along with cardiac biomarker assessment (eg, troponins) to evaluate myocardial injury. Early&#x000a0;transthoracic echocardiography can help detect wall motion abnormalities, ventricular wall rupture, or other structural cardiac complications. Patients identified as high risk should be closely monitored in an intensive care unit setting with serial hemodynamic assessments to facilitate early detection of complications such as VSR.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r20">[20]</xref></p>
        <p>A key preventive strategy is timely reperfusion therapy, including thrombolysis, percutaneous coronary intervention, or coronary artery bypass grafting, which significantly reduces the risk of myocardial rupture by limiting infarct size and myocardial necrosis.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r9">[9]</xref> In addition, optimization of evidence-based medical therapy,&#x000a0;including beta-blockers, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitors or angiotensin receptor blockers, antiplatelet therapy, and lipid-lowering agents,&#x000a0;plays an important role in stabilizing myocardial injury and preventing further ischemic complications.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r21">[21]</xref> Management of patients at risk for VSR also benefits from a multidisciplinary heart team approach that involves&#x000a0;cardiologists, cardiothoracic surgeons, and intensivists. This collaborative approach allows individualized treatment planning, appropriate timing of intervention, and comprehensive monitoring of high-risk patients.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r18">[18]</xref></p>
        <p>Patient education is equally important in reducing the risk of delayed myocardial infarction presentation and subsequent complications such as VSR. Patients should be educated about early symptoms of myocardial infarction, including chest pain, shortness of breath, diaphoresis, and unexplained fatigue, and encouraged to seek immediate medical attention if these symptoms occur. Lifestyle modification should be strongly emphasized to reduce overall cardiovascular risk, including smoking cessation, adherence to a heart-healthy diet, regular physical activity tailored to the individual, weight management, and stress reduction.</p>
        <p>Patients should also be counseled on the importance of strict adherence to prescribed medications, including antihypertensives, antiplatelet agents, and lipid-lowering therapies. Regular follow-up visits are essential to monitor clinical status, optimize medical therapy, and detect early signs of cardiac dysfunction or complications. Diagnostic surveillance with echocardiography or stress testing may also be used when appropriate to evaluate cardiac function and guide long-term management.<xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r22">[22]</xref><xref ref-type="bibr" rid="article-27468.r23">[23]</xref></p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s14" sec-type="Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes ">
        <title>Enhancing Healthcare Team Outcomes </title>
        <p>Effective management of postinfarction VSR requires rapid recognition and coordinated interprofessional intervention. Physicians, advanced practitioners, and nurses must maintain a high index of suspicion for mechanical complications in patients with recent myocardial infarction who develop new murmurs, hemodynamic instability, or worsening heart failure. Early diagnostic evaluation with echocardiography and continuous hemodynamic monitoring, and prompt stabilization with vasoactive medications or mechanical circulatory support, are critical skills. Strategic clinical decision-making involves determining the optimal timing of surgical or percutaneous repair while balancing hemodynamic stability and the risk of ongoing myocardial injury. Pharmacists contribute by optimizing vasoactive medications, antiplatelet therapy, and anticoagulation management, while also monitoring for drug interactions and adverse effects in critically ill patients.</p>
        <p>Strong interprofessional communication and care coordination are essential to improving patient outcomes in this high-risk condition. Collaboration among cardiologists, cardiothoracic surgeons, intensivists, advanced practitioners, nurses, and imaging specialists enables timely diagnosis, appropriate procedural planning, and continuous reassessment of patient status. Nurses play a central role in monitoring for clinical deterioration, managing invasive lines and mechanical support devices, and providing patient and family education. Effective team communication ensures rapid escalation of care, alignment of treatment goals, and safe transitions between the catheterization laboratory, operating room, and intensive care unit. This coordinated, patient-centered approach enhances patient safety, supports informed decision-making, and improves overall team performance in the management of postinfarction VSR.</p>
      </sec>
      <sec id="article-27468.s15">
        <title>Review Questions</title>
        <list list-type="bullet">
          <list-item>
